Day 2 Session 1 — Process Management
The mental model first

A process is a running program. But from the kernel's perspective, it's a collection of resources: memory, open files, CPU time, and state. Everything the kernel knows about a process lives in /proc/<pid>/.

This is the "everything is a file" model from Day 1 applied to processes.

What a process actually is in memory

When a program runs, the kernel carves out a virtual address space for it:

High addresses
  Kernel space     ← process can't touch this directly, uses syscalls
  Stack            ← function calls, local variables (grows downward)
  ↓
  (gap)
  ↑
  Heap             ← dynamic allocation (malloc, Python objects)
  Data segment     ← global/static variables
  Text segment     ← the actual program code (read-only)
Low addresses

SRE relevance: when you see a process consuming growing memory over time, it's usually the heap growing — a memory leak. The stack rarely causes this.

Process states

Run this and look at the STAT column:

bash
ps aux

The states you'll see:

State	Meaning	SRE relevance
R	Running or runnable	Normal
S	Sleeping (interruptible)	Normal, waiting for something
D	Uninterruptible sleep	Waiting for I/O — cannot be killed
Z	Zombie	Process finished, parent hasn't read exit status
T	Stopped	Paused (Ctrl+Z or SIGSTOP)

D state is critical. If you see many D-state processes, the system is stuck waiting on I/O — usually a disk or NFS problem. SIGKILL does nothing. You wait for the I/O to complete or reboot.

Zombie processes aren't dangerous by themselves — they consume no CPU or memory, just a PID slot. But if you have thousands, you'll exhaust PIDs and can't create new processes. Root cause is always the parent process not calling wait().

Lab — process inspection
bash
# All processes
ps aux

# What each column means:
# USER    — who owns the process
# PID     — process ID
# %CPU    — CPU usage
# %MEM    — memory usage
# VSZ     — virtual memory size (kb) — includes everything mapped, not all in RAM
# RSS     — resident set size (kb) — actual RAM currently used. This is what matters.
# STAT    — process state
# START   — when it started
# TIME    — total CPU time consumed
# COMMAND — what command started it

# Top 10 by CPU
ps aux | sort -k3 -rn | head -10

# Top 10 by memory
ps aux | sort -k4 -rn | head -10

# Process tree — see parent-child relationships
ps -ef --forest

# Your current shell's process info
cat /proc/$$/status

# Memory map of your shell
cat /proc/$$/maps

Look at the output of cat /proc/$$/status. You'll see fields like:

Name:   bash
State:  S (sleeping)
Pid:    1234
PPid:   1233        ← parent PID
VmRSS:  4532 kB    ← actual RAM used
VmSize: 15432 kB   ← virtual memory
Threads: 1

$$ is a bash variable that holds your current shell's PID. This trick works anywhere.

Real-time monitoring
bash
# top — the classic
top
# Key columns: PR (priority), NI (nice value), VIRT, RES, SHR, S (state)
# Press: M to sort by memory, P to sort by CPU, k to kill, q to quit
# Press 1 to see per-CPU breakdown

# htop — more readable
htop
# F5 for tree view, F6 to sort, F9 to kill

# Watch top 5 CPU consumers, refresh every second
watch -n 1 'ps aux | sort -k3 -rn | head -6'
Signals

Signals are how you communicate with a running process. The process either handles the signal, ignores it, or gets terminated by it.

bash
# Send SIGTERM (graceful stop — process can clean up)
kill -15 <PID>
# or
kill <PID>      # SIGTERM is the default

# Send SIGKILL (immediate, cannot be caught or ignored)
kill -9 <PID>

# Send SIGHUP (reload config — many daemons listen for this)
kill -1 <PID>

# List all signals
kill -l

The operational difference:

SIGTERM → process receives it, can finish current requests, close connections, flush buffers, then exit. This is what systemctl stop sends first.

SIGKILL → kernel terminates the process immediately. No cleanup. Open files may be left in inconsistent state. Use only when SIGTERM doesn't work.

In practice: always try SIGTERM first. Wait 5-10 seconds. If still running, then SIGKILL.

bash
# Lab — see this yourself
sleep 1000 &        # start a background process, note the PID printed
jobs                # see background jobs
kill -15 <PID>      # try SIGTERM
ps aux | grep sleep # confirm it's gone

sleep 1000 &
kill -9 <PID>       # SIGKILL
ps aux | grep sleep # gone immediately
nice and renice — CPU priority

Nice value ranges from -20 (highest priority, gets more CPU) to +19 (lowest priority, gets least CPU). Default is 0.

Regular users can only lower priority (increase nice value). Root can do both.

bash
# Start a process with low priority
nice -n 10 sleep 100 &

# Check the NI column in ps
ps aux | grep sleep

# Change priority of a running process
sudo renice -n -5 -p <PID>

# In top: the NI column shows nice value, PR shows actual kernel priority (PR = 20 + NI)

SRE relevance: if a batch job is competing with your web service for CPU, you renice the batch job to a higher nice value so the web service gets priority.

Script task: process_monitor.sh

Write this and commit it to GitHub under sre-prep-lab/scripts/:

bash
#!/bin/bash
# process_monitor.sh
# Shows system process health: top consumers, zombies, load average
# Usage: ./process_monitor.sh

echo "============================================"
echo " PROCESS MONITOR — $(hostname) — $(date)"
echo "============================================"

echo ""
echo "--- Load Average ---"
cat /proc/loadavg
# Format: 1min 5min 15min running/total lastpid
# Rule: if 1min avg > nproc, system is overloaded
NPROC=$(nproc)
echo "CPU count: $NPROC"

echo ""
echo "--- Top 5 CPU Consumers ---"
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | awk 'NR==1 || NR<=6 {print}'

echo ""
echo "--- Top 5 Memory Consumers ---"
ps aux --sort=-%mem | awk 'NR==1 || NR<=6 {print}'

echo ""
echo "--- Zombie Processes ---"
ZOMBIES=$(ps aux | awk '$8=="Z" {print $0}')
if [ -z "$ZOMBIES" ]; then
    echo "None found."
else
    echo "$ZOMBIES"
fi

echo ""
echo "--- High CPU Alert (> 80%) ---"
ps aux | awk 'NR>1 && $3>80 {print "WARNING: PID "$2" ("$11") using "$3"% CPU"}'
Troubleshooting scenario

"A service randomly stops. You don't know why."

This is the framework — in order, no skipping:

bash
# Step 1: Check the service status and last logs
systemctl status <service>
journalctl -u <service> --since "1 hour ago"
# Look for: exit code, signal that killed it, last log lines before death

# Step 2: What was the exit code?
# Exit 0 = clean exit (someone stopped it intentionally)
# Exit 1 = error
# Exit 137 = killed by signal 9 (SIGKILL) — often OOM killer
# Exit 143 = killed by signal 15 (SIGTERM) — systemd or another process stopped it

# Step 3: Check if OOM killer fired
dmesg -T | grep -i "killed process"
journalctl -k | grep -i "out of memory"

# Step 4: Check resource limits
systemctl show <service> | grep -E "Memory|CPU|Limit"

# Step 5: Check dependencies — did something it depends on fail first?
systemctl list-dependencies <service>

# Step 6: Did the config change?
journalctl -u <service> -b -1
# -b -1 = logs from the PREVIOUS boot (critical if system rebooted)

The exit code is your first real clue. 137 almost always means OOM. 143 means something sent SIGTERM. 1 means the process itself errored — check the logs for the actual error message.

The connection to yesterday

Yesterday: /proc is a live kernel interface.

Today: every process has its own directory in /proc/<pid>/ — that's where all this state lives. ps and top just read from /proc and format it nicely. You can always go directly:

bash
cat /proc/<pid>/status      # state, memory, PIDs
cat /proc/<pid>/cmdline | tr '\0' ' '    # full command that started the process
ls /proc/<pid>/fd           # open file descriptors
cat /proc/<pid>/limits      # resource limits (open files, max processes, etc.)

This matters in production when tools aren't available. /proc is always there.
